  <h1>India’s Gender Inequality in Workforce — Barriers and Solutions</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Despite progress in education and political participation, India’s female labour force participation rate (FLFPR) remains among the lowest in the world. In 2024, The Hindu analysed structural barriers to women’s workforce participation, from unpaid care work to workplace discrimination. For UPSC, this issue reflects the convergence of social justice, economy, and gender equality.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	FLFPR (2024): ~24% (ILO data).<br/>•	Urban Women Employment: Declining in formal sector jobs.<br/>•	Wage Gap: Women earn ~30% less than men on average.<br/>•	Unpaid Work: Women spend ~5 hours/day on unpaid care, vs 1 hour for men.<br/>•	Representation: Only ~17% in senior management roles.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Barriers to Participation<br/>1.	Social Norms: Patriarchal attitudes restrict mobility.<br/>2.	Care Burden: Lack of childcare and eldercare infrastructure.<br/>3.	Safety Concerns: Violence and harassment limit opportunities.<br/>4.	Skill Gap: Education not translating into employable skills.<br/>5.	Workplace Discrimination: Pay gaps, glass ceiling, maternity bias.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Government Measures<br/>•	Maternity Benefit (Amendment) Act, 2017: 26 weeks paid leave.<br/>•	POSH Act (2013): Workplace harassment prevention.<br/>•	Skill India & Stand Up India: Women-focused skilling and entrepreneurship.<br/>•	Self-Help Groups (SHGs): 80+ lakh SHGs under DAY-NRLM.<br/>•	Beti Bachao Beti Padhao: Education and awareness campaigns.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>•	SHG Revolution: Women-led collectives improving incomes and bargaining power.<br/>•	Startups & Gig Economy: More women entrepreneurs entering.<br/>•	Political Empowerment: 1/3rd reservation in Parliament & Assemblies (2023).<br/>________________________________________<br/>Challenges<br/>•	Implementation Gaps: POSH and maternity provisions poorly enforced in informal sector.<br/>•	Urban Bias: Rural women lack access to formal jobs.<br/>•	Digital Divide: Women underrepresented in digital economy.<br/>•	Low Female STEM Presence: <20% women in high-tech roles.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Economic Growth: Equal participation could add $770 billion to GDP (McKinsey).<br/>•	Social Justice: Women’s work linked to dignity and equality.<br/>•	Political Stability: Gender equity strengthens democracy.<br/>•	Global Standing: India’s progress on SDG 5 (Gender Equality) crucial.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-I (Society): Role of women, gender inequality.<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Policies for empowerment.<br/>•	GS-III (Economy): Employment and growth.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“No country can progress if half its population is left behind.”<br/>o	“Women’s empowerment is the key to inclusive development.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Feminist perspectives and political economy.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Childcare Infrastructure: Public-funded creches, workplace daycare.<br/>2.	Safe Mobility: Better policing, transport, street lighting.<br/>3.	Skill Development: STEM training, digital skilling for women.<br/>4.	Workplace Reforms: Flexible work, equal pay enforcement.<br/>5.	Mindset Change: Campaigns to shift patriarchal norms.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>Women’s workforce participation is not just an economic issue but a social transformation imperative. India must combine policy, infrastructure, and cultural change to unlock its full demographic potential. For UPSC aspirants, this reflects the intersection of economy, social justice, and gender equity.</p>
